    "text": "ORGANIZATIONAL PROFILE AND OPERATING SNAPSHOT\n\n\n\nPrepared by [STAFF_1], Executive Director. 2026-04-08. Internal reference document, updated annually, used to populate funder applications.\n\n§1. Identification\nLegal name: Northbridge Youth Mental Health Society. Operating name: Northbridge Youth. Incorporated under the Societies Act (Alberta), 14 June 2011. Registered charity, CRA business number [BN_1]. Organization type: not-for-profit. Category: community-based social services. Primary address [ADDRESS_1]. Mailing address same as primary. Telephone [PHONE_1]. General email [EMAIL_1].\n\n§2. Mission and mandate\nNorthbridge exists so that a young person in north-central Edmonton can reach a counsellor on the day they decide to ask. The Society delivers free, no-referral mental health support to youth aged 12 to 24 and structured support to the adults raising them. All services are free at point of use. No diagnosis, no health card, and no parental consent are required for youth 16 and over.\n\n§3. Leadership and governance\nThe Society is Black-led. All four members of the senior management team and seven of the nine directors identify as Black, of African and Caribbean origin. Leadership composition is reviewed annually by the Governance Committee and recorded in the minutes. Board of nine directors, meeting six times per year. Senior management: Executive Director [STAFF_1], Director of Programs [STAFF_2], Finance Manager [STAFF_3], Clinical Lead [STAFF_4].\n\n§4. Communities served\nService area is north-central Edmonton, principally the neighbourhoods served by [SCHOOL_1] through [SCHOOL_5]. In FY2025-26, youth of African origin represented 44 percent of participants across all programs and youth of Caribbean origin 9 percent, together the largest share of the participant population. First-generation newcomer youth were 38 percent of the total across all programs and 61 percent within Pathfinder. Languages spoken by participants at home, in order of frequency: English, Arabic, Tigrinya, Somali, Amharic, Dari, Tagalog, Spanish.\n\n§5. Programs\nBridge, single-session walk-in counselling, operating since 2013, no appointment or referral required, Monday to Friday 11:00 to 19:00.\nAnchor, an eight-week structured group program delivered inside five partner schools, operating since 2016.\nPathfinder, one-to-one navigation and case management with a newcomer youth focus, operating since 2019.\nKinship, a caregiver support pilot launched July 2025, seven-session cycle, funded to March 2027.\n\n§6. Staffing\n19 staff, 14.2 FTE, as of 2026-03-31. Six counsellors (5.6 FTE), four navigators (3.4 FTE), two group facilitators (1.6 FTE), one intake and data coordinator, four administrative and management, two summer students under [FUNDER_5] and Canada Summer Jobs. Detail in document 12.\n\n§7. Facility\n2,140 square feet, leased from [LANDLORD_1] since 2017. Current lease term runs to 2029-08-31. The space contains one group room, two enclosed private rooms, an open work area with six workstations, a reception area, and a shared washroom. Six counsellors and four navigators share the two private rooms. Facility limitations are documented in document 14 and referenced in documents 08, 09, 10 and 13.\n\n§8. Funders FY2025-26\n[FUNDER_1] City of Edmonton FCSS. [FUNDER_2] Alberta Health Services, Addiction and Mental Health. [FUNDER_3] United Way of the Alberta Capital Region. [FUNDER_4] Kettleridge Family Foundation. [FUNDER_5] Alberta Blue Cross Built Together. Canada Summer Jobs. Five fee-for-service school contracts. Individual and corporate donations.\n\n§9. Outcome measurement\nThe Society uses the Youth Wellbeing Scale, a five-item self-report measure scored 5 to 25 with higher scores indicating better wellbeing, administered at first contact and at program exit. Adopted 2022. Caregiver programming uses an eight-item satisfaction measure scored 8 to 32. Post-measure completion varies substantially by program and is discussed in each program report.",
