    "id": "02",
    "title": "Bridge Walk-In Counselling, Annual Program Report",
    "org": "[ORG_1]",
    "author": "[STAFF_4]",
    "authorType": "staff",
    "dateStart": "2026-04-14",
    "pages": 3,
    "text": "BRIDGE WALK-IN COUNSELLING, ANNUAL PROGRAM REPORT FY2025-26\n\n\n\nPrepared by [STAFF_4], Clinical Lead. 2026-04-14. Covers 2025-04-01 to 2026-03-31.\n\n§1. Program description\nBridge provides single-session counselling with no appointment, no referral and no waitlist. A young person walks in, gives a first name, and is seen the same day. Sessions run 45 to 60 minutes. Youth may return as often as they choose. Operating hours Monday to Friday, 11:00 to 19:00, 244 operating days in the reporting year.\n\n§2. Volume\n\n| Measure | FY2024-25 | FY2025-26 | Change |\n------|\n| Counselling contacts | 854 | 1,204 | +41.0% |\n| Unique youth | 476 | 612 | +28.6% |\n| Contacts per operating day | 3.5 | 4.9 | +40.0% |\n| Return visits as share of contacts | 44% | 49% | +5 pts |\n| Counsellor FTE assigned | 3.4 | 3.4 | no change |\n\n§3. Demand distribution\n61 percent of walk-in demand arrives Thursday and Friday between 14:00 and 19:00. Monday mornings run at 34 percent of capacity. The program turned away or asked to reschedule 88 youth during the year, all of them on Thursday or Friday afternoons. 31 of those 88 did not return within 60 days.\n\n§4. Age\nAges 12 to 14 accounted for 27 percent of Bridge contacts, up from 19 percent. This age band has no follow-on program at the Society. Anchor is delivered in senior high settings only and Pathfinder's referral pathway is built for youth 16 and over.\n\n§5. Outcomes\nThe Youth Wellbeing Scale is administered at first contact. Post-measures are attempted by text message 14 days after the last recorded contact.\n\n| Measure | Result |\n---|\n| Pre-measures completed | 1,109 of 1,204 contacts (92%) |\n| Post-measures completed | 265 of 1,204 contacts (22%) |\n| Mean pre score, matched pairs | 9.8 |\n| Mean post score, matched pairs | 13.6 |\n| Mean change, matched pairs | +3.8 |\n| Youth reporting they would return | 94% of respondents |\n\n§6. The measurement problem, stated plainly\nBridge is the Society's largest program by volume and produces the weakest outcome evidence. A single-session drop-in model has no natural exit point, so there is no moment at which a post-measure is owed. The 22 percent who answer the follow-up text are self-selected and are likely the youth who found it helpful. Northbridge should not represent the +3.8 change as a program-wide effect and has not done so in any funder report. This is the single largest gap in the Society's ability to evidence its own work.\n\n§7. Space\nTwo enclosed private rooms are available to six counsellors. On 14 recorded occasions during the year a Bridge session was held in the group room, the reception area or the open work area because no private room was free. Six of those 14 are logged in document 13. Counsellors have raised this at team meeting in six of eleven months.\n\n§8. Staffing note\nThree of the four staff departures recorded in FY2025-26 were Bridge counsellors. Exit interview themes are recorded in document 12.\n\n§9. Cost\nDirect and allocated program cost $402,000. Cost per contact $334, down from $423 in FY2024-25, a reduction of 21 percent. This reduction reflects rising volume against flat staffing rather than any efficiency gain, and should not be reported to funders as an efficiency.",
