    "id": "03",
    "title": "Anchor School Group Program, Annual Program Report",
    "org": "[ORG_1]",
    "author": "[STAFF_6]",
    "authorType": "staff",
    "dateStart": "2026-04-14",
    "pages": 3,
    "text": "ANCHOR SCHOOL GROUP PROGRAM, ANNUAL PROGRAM REPORT FY2025-26\n\n\n\nPrepared by [STAFF_6], Anchor Program Lead. 2026-04-14.\n\n§1. Program description\nAnchor is an eight-week structured group program delivered inside partner schools during and after school hours. Groups run 8 to 12 participants. Content covers emotion regulation, sleep, conflict, and help-seeking. Delivered at [SCHOOL_1] through [SCHOOL_5] under fee-for-service agreements and FCSS funding.\n\n§2. Volume\n\n| Measure | FY2024-25 | FY2025-26 | Change |\n------|\n| Groups scheduled | 28 | 24 | -14.3% |\n| Groups delivered | 26 | 18 | -30.8% |\n| Groups cancelled for under-enrolment | 2 | 6 | +200% |\n| Youth enrolled | 297 | 214 | -27.9% |\n| Youth completing 6 of 8 sessions | 241 (81%) | 167 (78%) | -3 pts |\n| Partner schools | 5 | 5 | no change |\n\n§3. Cancellations\nSix groups were cancelled for under-enrolment: two at [SCHOOL_2] in the autumn term, one at [SCHOOL_3], and three at [SCHOOL_5] in the winter term. In each case fewer than five youth had registered at the two-week mark. [SCHOOL_5] has now had three consecutive cancellations and the partnership should be reviewed before the FY2026-27 agreements are signed.\n\n§4. What school partners report\nCounsellors at three of the five schools report that youth increasingly ask to be referred to Bridge rather than join a group. Two schools attribute this to timetable pressure. One names group format directly, reporting that students describe an eight-week commitment as a bigger step than a walk-in.\n\n§5. Outcomes\n\n| Measure | FY2024-25 | FY2025-26 |\n---\n| Pre-measures completed | 289 (97%) | 209 (98%) |\n| Post-measures completed | 232 (78%) | 167 (78%) |\n| Mean pre score | 11.6 | 11.4 |\n| Mean post score | 15.9 | 15.8 |\n| Mean change | +4.3 | +4.4 |\n| Participants improving by 3 or more points | 71% | 73% |\n\nAnchor produces the Society's strongest outcome evidence by a wide margin. Because the program has a fixed eight-week structure, a defined final session, and a facilitator in the room, post-measure completion sits at 78 percent and has been stable for three years.\n\n§6. Cost\nDirect and allocated program cost $438,000. Cost per enrolled participant $2,047, up from $1,397 in FY2024-25, an increase of 47 percent. The rise is driven by fixed facilitation and preparation cost spread across fewer delivered groups. Six cancelled groups carried an estimated $34,000 in unrecovered preparation and facilitation time.\n\n§7. Note for the Board\nAnchor receives the largest share of program expenditure and is the only program the Society can evidence well. Its demand is falling. Bridge and Pathfinder demand is rising and neither can be evidenced to the same standard. That combination should be treated as the central planning question for FY2026-27.",
