    "id": "04",
    "title": "Pathfinder Youth Navigation, Annual Program Report",
    "org": "[ORG_1]",
    "author": "[STAFF_5]",
    "authorType": "staff",
    "dateStart": "2026-04-16",
    "pages": 3,
    "text": "PATHFINDER YOUTH NAVIGATION, ANNUAL PROGRAM REPORT FY2025-26\n\n\n\nPrepared by [STAFF_5], Pathfinder Team Lead. 2026-04-16.\n\n§1. Program description\nPathfinder pairs a youth aged 16 to 24 with a navigator for up to six months. Navigators support access to housing, income, school re-entry, immigration and settlement processes, primary care, and specialist mental health services. Referrals come from schools, Bridge, settlement agencies, and self-referral. Funded by [FUNDER_3] and [FUNDER_4].\n\n§2. Volume\n\n| Measure | FY2024-25 | FY2025-26 | Change |\n------|\n| Youth served | 96 | 148 | +54.2% |\n| Referrals received | 121 | 214 | +76.9% |\n| Navigator FTE | 3.4 | 3.4 | no change |\n| Active caseload per navigator, year end | 21 | 34 | +61.9% |\n| Waitlist, year end | 11 | 34 | +209% |\n| Mean days from referral to first contact | 3.2 | 11.6 | +263% |\n| Referrals closing without first contact | 9 (7%) | 47 (22%) | +15 pts |\n\n§3. The drop-off\n47 young people were referred to Pathfinder in FY2025-26 and never had a first contact. 34 of those 47 were unreachable after the fourth outreach attempt. The mean wait for that group was 14.1 days. The mean wait for youth who did attend a first contact was 9.8 days. Youth who wait longer are less likely to arrive.\n\n§4. Population\nFirst-generation newcomer youth were 61 percent of the Pathfinder caseload, up from 38 percent in FY2024-25. Countries of origin most represented: Eritrea, Somalia, Sudan, Syria, Afghanistan, Nigeria, Philippines.\n\n§5. Language\nInterpretation was requested for 63 of 148 youth. Languages requested: Tigrinya (24), Somali (16), Arabic (11), Dari (7), Amharic (5). The Society can deliver service directly in Arabic through one navigator. All other language requests are met by purchased interpretation, by a family member, or not at all. Nine youth were served with a family member interpreting, which is documented here as a practice the Society should stop. The interpretation budget was $5,000 and actual spend was $17,000.\n\n§6. Outcomes\n\n| Measure | Result |\n---|\n| Pre-measures completed | 141 of 148 (95%) |\n| Post-measures completed | 95 of 148 (64%) |\n| Mean pre score | 10.2 |\n| Mean post score | 14.1 |\n| Mean change | +3.9 |\n| Youth with a stable housing outcome at exit | 71 of 95 measured (75%) |\n| Youth re-enrolled in school or training at exit | 44 of 95 measured (46%) |\n\n§7. Cost\nDirect and allocated program cost $246,000. Cost per youth served $1,662, down from $2,146. As with Bridge, the reduction reflects caseload growth against flat staffing.\n\n§8. Team note\nNavigators carried a mean active caseload of 34 at year end against a program design assumption of 20. The team lead has raised this at three consecutive supervision meetings and in the email thread of November 2025.",
