    "id": "10",
    "title": "Internal email thread: waitlist and group cancellations",
    "org": "[ORG_1]",
    "author": "[STAFF_1], [STAFF_2], [STAFF_5]",
    "authorType": "staff",
    "dateStart": "2025-11-03",
    "pages": 2,
    "text": "INTERNAL EMAIL THREAD: WAITLIST AND GROUP CANCELLATIONS\n\n\n\nFour messages, 2025-11-03 to 2025-11-06.\n\n§1. 2025-11-03, 08:12. From [STAFF_5] to [STAFF_2].\nSubject: Pathfinder waitlist\nMorning. We are at 26 on the list this morning and my team is at 29 active each. Design assumption is 20. I have three youth who were referred in the second week of October and have not had a first contact yet. Two of them I have called four times.\nI am not asking for a new hire in November, I know where we are. I am asking whether we can close intake for two weeks so we can catch the people already waiting. Right now we are collecting names faster than we are meeting people, and a name on a list is not a service.\n\n§2. 2025-11-04, 17:40. From [STAFF_2] to [STAFF_5], copy [STAFF_1].\nSubject: RE: Pathfinder waitlist\nI hear you and I do not think I can approve closing intake. Two problems. The [FUNDER_3] agreement counts referrals received, not youth served, so closing intake shows up as a drop in a number they watch. And most of the October referrals came from schools, who will stop referring if we close and will not necessarily start again when we reopen.\nWhat I can do is put the two summer student hours onto outreach calls for the waiting list, and take the waitlist policy back to the Board in November with an actual outreach protocol attached.\nSeparate thing that is bothering me. We have six cancelled Anchor groups this year and a Pathfinder list of 26. Those are the same young people. They are telling us what they want and we are funded for the other thing.\n\n§3. 2025-11-05, 09:26. From [STAFF_1] to [STAFF_2] and [STAFF_5].\nSubject: RE: Pathfinder waitlist\nAgreed on not closing intake, and agreed on the bigger point.\nBeing blunt about the position we are in. Anchor is 34 percent of our program spend and it is the only program where I can put a number in front of a funder that I fully believe. Bridge post-measure completion is 22 percent. Pathfinder is 64 percent. If I move money from Anchor to Pathfinder next year I improve service and I weaken my reporting at the same time, and reporting is what renews the money.\nThat is the actual problem and it is not a scheduling problem. Let us get it in front of the Board properly in February with the year-end numbers rather than arguing it in email in November.\n\n§4. 2025-11-06, 07:55. From [STAFF_5] to [STAFF_1] and [STAFF_2].\nSubject: RE: Pathfinder waitlist\nUnderstood, and thank you for saying it that directly.\nOne thing for the February paper. Of the youth who dropped off the list before a first contact this year, the average wait was 14 days. For the ones who made it to a first contact it was under 10. The wait is not neutral. It is doing the selecting for us, and it is selecting against the young people who are hardest to reach.",
