    "text": "ANONYMISED CLIENT OUTCOME SUMMARIES\n\n\n\nPrepared by [STAFF_4] for the annual report and funder narrative. 2026-03-27. Marked by the Society as \"anonymised for external use.\"\n\n\nRedaction demo note: this is the document that proves the point. The Society\nbelieves this file is anonymous. Insyte finds 23 identifying spans in it.\n\n\n§1. Summary A\n[YOUTH_1], 16, referred to Bridge by a counsellor at [SCHOOL_2] in September 2025 after missing eleven days of school. Arrived at walk-in on a Thursday afternoon and waited 50 minutes because both rooms were occupied. Seen the same day. Presented with sleep disruption, low mood and conflict at home following his family's move to Edmonton from Calgary in July. Youngest of four. His older brother D. had attended Anchor at the same school in 2023. Attended Bridge six times between September and February and joined an Anchor group in January. Wellbeing score 8 at first contact and 15 at the Anchor post-measure. In his own words at the final session: \"the first time I came in I was only there because Ms. G made me. I came back the other times because it was the only place on 95 Street where nobody wanted anything from me.\"\n\n§2. Summary B\n[YOUTH_2], 19, self-referred to Pathfinder in August 2025, eleven days after the referral was received. Arrived in Canada in 2023, born 2006-05-14. Living with an aunt in Kilkenny. Requested support with school re-entry, a provincial identification card, and a family doctor. Interpretation in Tigrinya was required and was provided by her cousin for the first two sessions before purchased interpretation was arranged, which the navigator has recorded as an inappropriate practice. Wellbeing score 9 at intake and 14 at exit. Re-enrolled in a high school completion program in January 2026 and registered with a family clinic in February.\n\n§3. Summary C\n[YOUTH_3], 14, attended Bridge four times between November 2025 and February 2026, first attending after a friend brought her. Presented with anxiety about a court date on 2026-01-22 relating to a family matter, and with escalating conflict with her mother. She is 14, so no follow-on program was available to her: Anchor runs in senior high settings and Pathfinder takes referrals from 16. The Clinical Lead notes this as the clearest example of the age gap described in document 02 section 4. Wellbeing score 11 at first contact. No post-measure was completed, which is the norm for Bridge. The youth was still attending at year end.\n\n§4. Note on consent\nAll three young people gave verbal consent for an anonymised summary to be used in reporting. Consent was recorded in the case record. Written consent was not obtained. The Society's consent procedure does not currently distinguish between internal reporting and external publication.",
